-- title:
--   The anchor point and its tail-limit set
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $H_m$ be a decreasing family of sets of selfmaps of $K$ (the resolvent tails), and $x\in K$.
--
--   1. **Tail closures.** $\overline{\{h(x):h\in H_{n+1}\}}$, closed in the weak topology of $K$.
--   2. **Tail-limit set.**
--   $$
--   D_x=\bigcap_n\overline{\{h(x):h\in H_{n+1}\}}^{\,w}\subseteq K .
--   $$
--   3. **Anchor point.** A point $x$ such that $D_x$ is nonempty and weakly compact and $x\in\overline{\mathrm{conv}}\,D_x$ in norm. Every $d\in D_x$ is then a weak limit point of $\{h(x):h\in H_m\}$ for every $m\ge1$. For the resolvent tails an anchor exists: it is a point fixed by an idempotent of the tail semigroups.
